/-!
# Regular rings

A Noetherian ring `R` is *regular* if every localization `R_𝔭` at a prime `𝔭` of `R` is a regular
local ring, that is, a Noetherian local ring whose maximal ideal is generated by `dim R_𝔭`
elements. The definition does not clash with the notion already fixed for a Noetherian local ring
`R`: by Lemma 0AFS a Noetherian local ring is a regular local ring exactly when it is regular in
the above sense.

This predicate is Mathlib's class `IsRegularRing`, which is the source's sentence with nothing
added and nothing dropped: `IsNoetherianRing R` together with
`IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime p)` for every prime `p` of `R`. Its defining field,
however, is pinned to the concrete model `Localization.AtPrime p`, whereas the source speaks of
*the* localization `R_𝔭`, which may be any model of `R_𝔭`. This file supplies the definition in
that model-independent form: any ring `S` which is a localization of a regular ring `R` at a prime
`𝔭` — in any universe — is a regular local ring.

## Main results

* `IsRegularRing.isRegularLocalRing_of_isLocalization`: if `R` is a regular ring, `𝔭` is a prime of
  `R` and `S` is a localization of `R` at `𝔭`, then `S` is a regular local ring. This is the
  source's Definition 00OD read with an arbitrary model of `R_𝔭`.

## Implementation notes

No new predicate is introduced: `IsRegularRing` is already the source's definition verbatim, so a
second name for it would buy a citable name and cost every user a bridge. What the source's phrase
"the localizations `R_𝔭`" does carry beyond Mathlib's field is independence of the model, and that
is the content stated here; the converse direction, that regularity of `R` follows from the family
of conditions, is Mathlib's `isRegularRing_iff`, and Noetherianness is
`IsRegularRing.toIsNoetherian`. The statement is universe-polymorphic in `S`: the localization at a
prime of `R : Type u` may be presented in any universe, and `IsRegularLocalRing.of_ringEquiv`
transports regularity along `IsLocalization.algEquiv`, which is the whole proof.

The remark following the source's definition — that it suffices to require the local rings at
*maximal* ideals to be regular — is a lemma about the notion rather than part of it, and so is the
source's warning that regularity of a Noetherian ring is not finite global dimension (Lemma 00OE
with Lemma 0AFS); neither is stated here.

Faithfulness to the source, point by point. (1) No hypothesis is idle: `IsRegularRing R` is what is
being read, `p.IsPrime` is the source's quantifier and `IsLocalization.AtPrime S p` is what makes
`S` the object `R_𝔭` the source names; no dimension bound, no local or domain hypothesis on `R` is
added, and Noetherianness is not assumed separately because `IsRegularRing` already contains it.
(2) The scalars and base are the source's: the localization is of `R` itself at its own prime `p`
via `Algebra R S` with `IsLocalization.AtPrime S p`, and "regular local ring" is
`IsRegularLocalRing` — Tag 00KU, `(maximalIdeal S).spanFinrank = ringKrullDim S` — the very notion
the source refers back to. (3) The object the source names, the localization at a prime, is pinned
rather than merely asserted to exist: `S` is required to satisfy `IsLocalization.AtPrime S p`, so
the canonical map `algebraMap R S` is the source's `R → R_𝔭`, and the conclusion is about that `S`.
(4) The instantiation is not degenerate: `S = Localization.AtPrime p` is a witness for every
regular `R`, regular rings abound (every field, `ℤ`, every Dedekind domain by
`IsRegularRing` of a Dedekind domain), and the conclusion genuinely fails for a Noetherian
non-regular ring such as `k[x]/(x²)`, whose only localization is itself.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 00OD](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00OD)
-/

